=back

Returns boolean: true if the event is being processed, false otherwise.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_RegisterEvents

  my $result = SDL_RegisterEvents($numevents);

C: C<< Uint32 SDL_RegisterEvents(int numevents) >>

Allocate a set of user-defined events, and return the beginning event number for that set of events.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item numevents - integer. the number of events to be allocated.

=back

Returns unsigned integer: the beginning event number, or 0 if numevents is invalid or if there are not enough user-defined events left.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetWindowFromEvent

  my $result = SDL_GetWindowFromEvent($event);

C: C<< SDL_Window * SDL_GetWindowFromEvent(const SDL_Event * event) >>

Get window associated with an event.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item event - SDL3::Event object or undef. an event containing a `windowID`.

=back

Returns SDL3::Window object or undef: the associated window on success or NULL if there is none.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetEventDescription

  my $result = SDL_GetEventDescription($event, $buf, $buflen);

C: C<< int SDL_GetEventDescription(const SDL_Event * event, char * buf, int buflen) >>

Generate an English description of an event.

This will fill `buf` with a null-terminated string that might look something like this:

``` SDL_EVENT_MOUSE_MOTION (timestamp=1140256324 windowid=2 which=0 state=0 x=492.99 y=139.09 xrel=52 yrel=6) ```

The exact format of the string is not guaranteed; it is intended for logging purposes, to be read by a human, and not parsed by a computer.

The returned value follows the same rules as SDL_snprintf(): `buf` will always be NULL-terminated (unless `buflen` is zero), and will be truncated if `buflen` is too small. The return code is the number of bytes needed for the complete string, not counting the NULL-terminator, whether the string was truncated or not. Unlike SDL_snprintf(), though, this function never returns -1.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item event - SDL3::Event object or undef. an event to describe. May be NULL.

=item buf - writable string buffer. the buffer to fill with the description string. May be NULL.

=item buflen - integer. the maximum bytes that can be written to `buf`.

=back

Returns integer: number of bytes needed for the full string, not counting the null-terminator byte.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.4.0.


=head1 ENUMERATIONS

Every enumerator is exported as a constant sub.

